  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q19-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "19",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $a,b\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and that $x=\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}\\in\\mathbb{Q}$. Show that $x\\in\\mathbb{N}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x\\in\\mathbb{N}$.\n\n$x$ is a rational root of the monic integer polynomial $t^4-2(a+b)t^2+(a-b)^2$, so it is an integer. Since $x>0$, it is natural.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=(\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b})^2\\\\x^2&=a+b+2\\sqrt{ab}\\\\x^2-(a+b)&=2\\sqrt{ab}\\\\(x^2-(a+b))^2&=4ab\\\\x^4-2(a+b)x^2+(a+b)^2&=4ab\\\\x^4-2(a+b)x^2+(a-b)^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x$ is a rational root of the monic polynomial $P(t)=t^4-2(a+b)t^2+(a-b)^2$, which has integer coefficients. By the Integer Root Theorem, $x$ is an integer. If $a=b$, factor out $t^2$ and apply the theorem to $t^2-4a$, since $x>0$. Since $x=\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}>0$, $x\\in\\mathbb{N}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
